The final production index was then created:

CREATE UNIQUE INDEX expenses_recurring_occurrence_unique_idx
ON public.expenses (
    user_id,
    recurring_group_id,
    expense_date
);


The final index was verified through pg_indexes.

Final definition:

expenses_recurring_occurrence_unique_idx

CREATE UNIQUE INDEX
ON public.expenses
USING btree
(
    user_id,
    recurring_group_id,
    expense_date
)


There is NO WHERE clause on the final production index.


============================================================
8. WHY THE FINAL INDEX IS NOT PARTIAL
============================================================

The recurring generator uses:

onConflict:
"user_id,recurring_group_id,expense_date"


PostgreSQL requires a matching unique or exclusion constraint
for the ON CONFLICT target.

The original partial unique index:

UNIQUE (
    user_id,
    recurring_group_id,
    expense_date
)
WHERE recurring_group_id IS NOT NULL


was not compatible with the ON CONFLICT specification being
used by the Supabase generator.

This produced the following production error:

"there is no unique or exclusion constraint matching the
ON CONFLICT specification"


Therefore the partial index was removed.

The final normal unique index is compatible with:

onConflict:
"user_id,recurring_group_id,expense_date"


PostgreSQL's normal unique-index behavior allows multiple NULL
values for recurring_group_id.

Therefore ordinary non-recurring expenses with:

recurring_group_id = NULL

do not conflict with one another.

This allows the database to use the simpler and correct
non-partial uniqueness rule.


============================================================
9. FINAL OCCURRENCE IDENTITY
============================================================

The authoritative identity of a recurring occurrence is:

user_id
+
recurring_group_id
+
expense_date


This means:

ONE USER
+
ONE RECURRING GROUP
+
ONE EXPENSE DATE
=
ONE RECURRING OCCURRENCE


The database now enforces this invariant.


============================================================
10. IMPORTANT SOFT-DELETE BEHAVIOR
============================================================

The final unique index does NOT exclude:

is_deleted = true


Therefore deleted recurring occurrences remain part of the
uniqueness rule.

This is intentional and matches the Elite X recurring-expense
architecture.

Example:

If an occurrence for:

2026-09-13

is manually deleted:

is_deleted = true


the deleted row remains in the database.

The recurring generator still sees that occurrence as existing
and does not recreate it.

This preserves the existing rule:

DELETED RECURRING OCCURRENCES MUST NOT BE REGENERATED.


